# Vue security

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic security rules to Vue-specific
render-time and routing risks. Applies only to `*.vue` files; server-side
Node.js concerns (request validation, dependency risk) still come from
`ts-js-node`'s security rule.

## Template injection (XSS)

- Never bind `v-html` to a value that includes user-controlled or
  unsanitized content -- `v-html` renders raw HTML with no escaping, so a
  string containing `<script>`/`onerror=`/etc. executes. Sanitize with a
  library such as DOMPurify immediately before the `v-html` binding if raw
  HTML rendering is genuinely required, and keep the sanitize call next to
  the binding so it cannot be bypassed by a later edit.
- Prefer interpolation (`{{ value }}`) or bound attributes (`:title="value"`)
  over `v-html` whenever the content is plain text -- both are
  auto-escaped by Vue's compiler, `v-html` is not.
- Treat a dynamic `:href`/`:src` bound to user-controlled input as
  untrusted: reject or allowlist the scheme (`http:`/`https:`/`mailto:`)
  before binding it, since a `javascript:` URI in an anchor's `href` still
  executes on click.
- Never pass a user-controlled string into `render()`'s `h()` calls as a
  raw HTML string; `h()` treats string children as text, but a manually
  constructed VNode tree that re-injects raw markup reintroduces the same
  risk `v-html` has.

## Routing and SSR

- Validate and narrow a Vue Router dynamic segment (`route.params.id`)
  before using it in a query, redirect target, or `v-html`-rendered value
  -- a route param is attacker-controlled input, not a trusted internal
  value.
- In a server-rendered (Nuxt or custom SSR) app, never read a secret,
  session token, or unsanitized request header directly into a `ref`/
  `reactive()` value that gets serialized into the client-hydrated state --
  anything in the initial state payload is visible in the page source.
- Guard a navigation guard's redirect target (`next('/somewhere')` or
  `router.push()`) built from a query parameter against open-redirect: only
  redirect to a same-origin, allowlisted path, never an unvalidated
  `redirect` query value passed straight through.

## Store and state exposure

- Do not store a raw auth token or other secret in a Pinia store field
  that is included in a `persist`/localStorage plugin without explicit
  review -- persisted store state survives in the browser's storage past
  the session and is readable by any script with DOM access on that
  origin.
- Scope a Pinia store's `$reset()` (or manual reset) to run on logout for
  any store holding user-specific data, so a shared browser session (or a
  test suite reusing app state) does not leak the previous user's data
  into the next session.
